-- Q1: medium / L2 — Premium Tax Credit reconciliation on return
(3, 'Individual Taxation: Credits', 'Garrett, a single taxpayer, receives advance premium tax credits of $4,800 during 2026 to help pay for a marketplace health insurance plan. At year-end, his actual income is higher than estimated, and the recalculated credit is $3,600. Under Section 36B, what happens when Garrett files his return?', '["Garrett keeps the full $4,800 and owes no additional tax", "Garrett must repay the $1,200 excess advance credit on his return", "The IRS automatically adjusts the excess with no action by Garrett", "Garrett loses the entire $4,800 credit and must repay it all"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under Section 36B, the premium tax credit is reconciled on the tax return. If advance payments exceed the actual credit, the excess must be repaid (subject to repayment caps based on income). Garrett repays $4,800 − $3,600 = $1,200. Unlike losing the entire credit, only the excess is repaid. Taxpayers who received no advance credits and are owed more can claim the difference as a refund.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q2: hard / L4 — Saver's Credit cliff threshold rate tiers
(3, 'Individual Taxation: Credits', 'Tanaka, a single taxpayer age 35 with AGI of $22,500, contributes $2,000 to her traditional IRA in 2026. The Saver''s Credit rate under Section 25B is 50% for single filers with AGI up to $23,000, 20% for AGI from $23,001 to $25,000, and 10% for AGI from $25,001 to $38,250. Evaluate why Tanaka''s $500 AGI increase to $23,500 would reduce her credit from $1,000 to $400.', '["The credit phases out gradually, reducing by $1 for each $10 of excess AGI", "The credit percentage drops incrementally as AGI increases within each tier", "The credit rate drops from 50% to 20% at the cliff threshold, cutting the credit amount by more than half", "The credit is recalculated using a blended rate of the two applicable tiers"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under Section 25B, the Saver''s Credit uses cliff thresholds rather than gradual phase-outs. At $22,500 AGI, Tanaka qualifies for the 50% rate ($2,000 × 50% = $1,000). If her AGI increases by $500 to $23,500, she drops to the 20% tier ($2,000 × 20% = $400). Unlike most phase-outs that reduce credits proportionally, the Saver''s Credit jumps between discrete rate brackets, making small income changes highly impactful.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q3: hard / L4 — Care credit lower-earning spouse income limit
(3, 'Individual Taxation: Credits', 'Burke and Diaz (MFJ) pay $12,000 in daycare for their two children ages 2 and 5 in 2026. Burke earns $45,000 and Diaz earns $4,000 part-time. Their AGI is $49,000. Evaluate the interaction between the expense limits under Section 21 and determine the maximum child and dependent care credit.', '["$800", "$1,200", "$1,000", "$6,000"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under Section 21, qualifying expenses are limited to the lower-earning spouse''s income ($4,000) and capped at $6,000 for two qualifying individuals. Diaz''s $4,000 income is the binding constraint. At $49,000 AGI, the credit rate is 20%. The credit is $4,000 × 20% = $800. Many candidates apply the $6,000 cap without recognizing the lower-earner limitation, which would incorrectly yield $1,200 ($6,000 × 20%).', 'hard', 4),

-- Q4: easy / L2 — EIC married filing separately restriction
(3, 'Individual Taxation: Credits', 'Valdez, a married taxpayer with two qualifying children and earned income of $30,000, files as married filing separately in 2026. Valdez applies for the earned income credit. Under Section 32, what is the result?', '["Valdez receives the full EIC for two qualifying children", "Valdez receives a reduced EIC based on the MFS phase-out schedule", "Valdez receives half the EIC amount since only one spouse is filing", "Valdez is ineligible for the earned income credit"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under Section 32(d), a taxpayer filing as married filing separately is generally disqualified from claiming the earned income credit. This restriction applies regardless of income level or number of qualifying children. Unlike other credits that merely reduce amounts for MFS filers, the EIC completely bars MFS taxpayers. To claim the EIC, married taxpayers must file jointly.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q5: medium / L2 — ACTC earned income threshold ($2,500)
(3, 'Individual Taxation: Credits', 'Russo has one qualifying child under age 17 and a tax liability of $500 before credits in 2026. Russo''s earned income is $8,000. Under Section 24, after applying the nonrefundable portion of the child tax credit, what is the refundable additional child tax credit?', '["$1,500", "$825", "$1,700", "$500"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under Section 24(d), the ACTC refundable portion equals 15% of earned income exceeding $2,500, capped at $1,700. Russo''s nonrefundable CTC reduces liability from $500 to $0 (using $500 of the $2,000 credit). The remaining $1,500 is potentially refundable. The ACTC is 15% × ($8,000 − $2,500) = 15% × $5,500 = $825. Since $825 is less than the $1,500 unused and less than $1,700, the ACTC is $825.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q6: medium / L2 — Adoption credit 5-year carryforward
(3, 'Individual Taxation: Credits', 'Nichols incurs $16,810 in qualified adoption expenses and claims the maximum adoption credit of $16,810 in 2026. Her regular tax liability before credits is $4,000. Under Section 23, how is the unused portion of the adoption credit handled?', '["The unused credit is refunded to Nichols", "The unused credit is permanently lost", "The unused credit carries forward for up to five years", "The unused credit carries back one year and forward ten years"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under Section 23(c), the adoption credit is nonrefundable, so it can reduce tax to zero but cannot generate a refund. Any excess carries forward for up to five years. Nichols uses $4,000 of the credit in 2026 and carries the remaining $12,810 forward. Unlike the foreign tax credit (1-back/10-forward) or general business credit (1-back/20-forward), the adoption credit has no carryback and only a five-year carryforward.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q7: hard / L4 — Education credit + 529 coordination no double benefit
(3, 'Individual Taxation: Credits', 'Kwan pays $8,000 in qualified tuition for her daughter''s first year of college in 2026. She also receives a $5,000 tax-free distribution from a 529 plan, all used for qualified education expenses. Kwan''s MAGI is $70,000 (single). Evaluate the interaction between the 529 exclusion and the AOTC under Section 25A and determine Kwan''s maximum credit.', '["$2,500", "$2,000", "$1,500", "$2,250"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under Section 25A(g)(2), expenses paid with tax-free 529 distributions cannot also be used for the AOTC. Of Kwan''s $8,000 tuition, $5,000 was covered by the 529 plan, leaving $3,000 in qualifying expenses for the AOTC. The credit is 100% of the first $2,000 ($2,000) plus 25% of the next $1,000 ($250), totaling $2,250. Many candidates claim the full $2,500 without reducing expenses by the 529 amount, violating the no-double-benefit rule.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q8: easy / L1 — CTC relationship test (foster child)
(3, 'Individual Taxation: Credits', 'Which of the following individuals qualifies as a qualifying child for the child tax credit under Section 24?', '["A taxpayer''s 10-year-old foster child placed by an authorized agency who lives with the taxpayer", "A taxpayer''s 18-year-old nephew who is not claimed as a dependent", "A taxpayer''s 15-year-old neighbor who lives with the taxpayer all year", "A taxpayer''s 20-year-old biological child who lives independently"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under Section 24(c) and Section 152(c), a qualifying child for the CTC must meet relationship, age, residency, and dependency tests. Foster children placed by authorized agencies satisfy the relationship test. The child must be under 17 at year-end, live with the taxpayer for more than half the year, and be claimed as a dependent. A neighbor fails the relationship test, and a non-dependent or over-age child does not qualify.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q9: hard / L3 — EIC fraud disqualification period (10 years)
(3, 'Individual Taxation: Credits', 'Martinez claimed the earned income credit in 2022 by fabricating self-employment income. The IRS determined the claim was fraudulent in 2024. Under Section 32(k), Martinez wants to claim the EIC for 2026 with legitimate earned income. Apply the disqualification rules to determine whether Martinez can claim the credit.', '["Martinez can claim the EIC because the fraud occurred more than two years ago", "Martinez cannot claim the EIC because the ten-year fraud disqualification extends through 2031", "Martinez can claim the EIC but must file Form 8862 and accept a reduced credit", "Martinez cannot claim the EIC because any prior denial permanently bars future claims"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under Section 32(k)(1)(B)(ii), a taxpayer whose EIC claim is denied due to fraud is disqualified from claiming the credit for ten years following the most recent tax year for which the determination was made. Since the fraud was for 2022, Martinez is barred through 2031. This is distinct from the two-year disqualification for reckless or intentional disregard (not fraud), and no permanent bar exists—it is specifically ten years for fraud.', 'hard', 3),

-- Q10: medium / L3 — CTC age test at end of tax year
(3, 'Individual Taxation: Credits', 'Anderson''s daughter turns 17 on December 28, 2026. Anderson claims her as a dependent and she has a valid SSN. Apply the child tax credit age requirement under Section 24 to determine whether Anderson qualifies for the $2,000 CTC for his daughter in 2026.', '["Yes, because she was under 17 for most of 2026", "Yes, because the age test is based on the child''s age at the start of the tax year", "No, because the child must be under age 17 at the end of the tax year", "No, because the child must be under age 17 on the date the return is filed"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under Section 24(c)(1), a qualifying child must be under age 17 at the close of the calendar year. Since Anderson''s daughter turns 17 on December 28, 2026, she is 17 at year-end and does not qualify for the $2,000 CTC. Anderson may instead claim the $500 credit for other dependents. The test is not based on the start of the year, the filing date, or the proportion of the year the child was under 17.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q11: hard / L4 — AMT minimum tax credit from prior ISO exercise
(3, 'Individual Taxation: Credits', 'Sato exercised incentive stock options in 2024, generating a $60,000 AMT adjustment that caused her to pay $15,000 in AMT above her regular tax. In 2026, Sato''s regular tax is $45,000 and her tentative minimum tax is $38,000. Evaluate Sato''s ability to use the minimum tax credit under Section 53 and determine the allowable credit.', '["$7,000", "$15,000", "$0", "$38,000"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under Section 53, a taxpayer who paid AMT due to deferral items (such as ISO exercises) generates a minimum tax credit that can offset regular tax in future years. The allowable credit equals the excess of regular tax over tentative minimum tax: $45,000 − $38,000 = $7,000. The remaining $8,000 ($15,000 − $7,000) carries forward. The full $15,000 cannot be used because the credit cannot reduce regular tax below tentative minimum tax.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q12: easy / L1 — Care credit imputed income for full-time student spouse
(3, 'Individual Taxation: Credits', 'Under Section 21, if one spouse is a full-time student with no earned income, what amount of monthly earned income is imputed for purposes of the child and dependent care credit?', '["$500 per month regardless of the number of qualifying individuals", "$1,000 per month for each qualifying individual", "No income is imputed; the student spouse must have actual earned income", "$250 per month if one qualifying individual, $500 per month if two or more"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under Section 21(d)(2), a spouse who is a full-time student is deemed to have earned income of $250 per month with one qualifying individual or $500 per month with two or more. This imputed income satisfies the requirement that both spouses have earned income to claim the care credit. The amounts are statutory and do not vary with the student spouse''s actual enrollment costs.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q13: medium / L3 — Education credits MFS filing status bar
(3, 'Individual Taxation: Credits', 'Crawford is married and files separately in 2026. She paid $6,000 in tuition for her graduate degree and wants to claim the Lifetime Learning Credit. Apply the filing status restrictions under Section 25A to determine Crawford''s eligibility.', '["Crawford may claim the full Lifetime Learning Credit of $1,200", "Crawford is ineligible for the Lifetime Learning Credit because MFS filers are excluded", "Crawford may claim a reduced Lifetime Learning Credit based on the MFS phase-out", "Crawford may claim the AOTC instead since the LLC is unavailable to graduate students"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under Section 25A(g)(6), both the American Opportunity Tax Credit and the Lifetime Learning Credit are completely unavailable to taxpayers who file as married filing separately. There is no reduced amount or alternative phase-out for MFS filers—the exclusion is absolute. This differs from credits like the child tax credit, which remain available to MFS filers. Crawford must file jointly to claim either education credit.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q14: medium / L2 — CTC phase-out rate ($50 per $1,000)
(3, 'Individual Taxation: Credits', 'Harper, a single parent with one qualifying child, has MAGI of $215,000 in 2026. The child tax credit begins phasing out at $200,000 for single filers. Under Section 24, the credit is reduced by $50 for each $1,000 (or fraction thereof) of MAGI over the threshold. What is Harper''s child tax credit?', '["$1,250", "$1,500", "$2,000", "$1,000"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under Section 24(b), the $2,000 CTC is reduced by $50 for each $1,000 (or fraction thereof) of MAGI exceeding $200,000 for single filers. Harper''s excess MAGI is $15,000, producing a phase-out reduction of 15 × $50 = $750. The credit is $2,000 − $750 = $1,250. A common error is applying the MFJ threshold of $400,000, which would result in no reduction.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q15: medium / L2 — FTC annual election between credit and deduction
(3, 'Individual Taxation: Credits', 'Monroe paid $8,000 in foreign income taxes during 2026. She itemizes deductions and considers whether to claim a foreign tax credit under Section 901 or a deduction under Section 164. Under the Internal Revenue Code, what constraint applies to this choice?', '["The election between credit and deduction is permanent once made", "The taxpayer must use the same method for at least three consecutive years", "The taxpayer may elect the credit or deduction each year independently", "The credit is mandatory if the foreign tax exceeds $1,000"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under Section 901, the choice between claiming a foreign tax credit or a foreign tax deduction is made annually and is not binding on future years. A taxpayer may credit foreign taxes one year and deduct them the next, depending on which provides greater benefit. However, the taxpayer cannot claim both a credit and a deduction for the same taxes in the same year.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q16: medium / L3 — Kiddie tax net unearned income calculation
(3, 'Individual Taxation: Credits', 'Patel, age 14, has $5,000 in dividend income and no earned income in 2026. The standard deduction for a dependent with only unearned income is $1,300. Under Section 1(g), the first $1,300 is offset by the standard deduction and the next $1,250 is taxed at the child''s rate. Apply the kiddie tax rules to determine how much of Patel''s income is taxed at the parent''s rate.', '["$5,000", "$3,700", "$1,250", "$2,450"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under Section 1(g), net unearned income subject to the kiddie tax is total unearned income minus the standard deduction ($1,300) and the next $1,250 taxed at the child''s rate. Patel''s calculation: $5,000 − $1,300 − $1,250 = $2,450 taxed at the parent''s marginal rate. The $3,700 figure ($5,000 − $1,300) incorrectly omits the $1,250 taxed at the child''s own rate.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q17: easy / L1 — AOTC two-tier calculation formula
(3, 'Individual Taxation: Credits', 'Under Section 25A(i), how is the American Opportunity Tax Credit calculated?', '["20% of the first $10,000 in qualified expenses", "100% of the first $2,000 plus 25% of the next $2,000 in qualified expenses", "50% of up to $5,000 in qualified expenses", "100% of up to $2,500 in qualified expenses"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under Section 25A(i), the AOTC equals 100% of the first $2,000 of qualified tuition and related expenses plus 25% of the next $2,000, for a maximum credit of $2,500 per eligible student. The 20% of $10,000 formula describes the Lifetime Learning Credit, not the AOTC. The two-tier calculation is a key distinction from other credits that apply a single percentage rate.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q18: medium / L2 — General business credit carryback/forward periods
(3, 'Individual Taxation: Credits', 'Patterson, a sole proprietor, generates $12,000 in general business credits during 2026 but can only use $8,000 against her tax liability. Under Section 39, what is the carryback and carryforward period for the $4,000 unused general business credit?', '["Carry back 1 year and carry forward 20 years", "Carry back 1 year and carry forward 10 years", "No carryback, carry forward 5 years", "Carry back 3 years and carry forward 15 years"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under Section 39(a), unused general business credits may be carried back one year and forward twenty years. This is more generous than the adoption credit (no carryback, 5-year carryforward) but differs from the foreign tax credit (1-year carryback, 10-year carryforward). Patterson carries the $4,000 excess back to 2025 first; any remaining amount carries forward through 2046.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q19: hard / L4 — Estimated tax penalty + withholding safe harbor analysis
(3, 'Individual Taxation: Credits', 'Chambers earns $250,000 in W-2 wages with $62,000 withheld and receives $100,000 in partnership income with no withholding in 2026. Her total tax liability is $75,000 and prior-year AGI was $180,000 with prior-year tax of $55,000. Evaluate whether Chambers owes an underpayment penalty under Section 6654, considering all safe harbors.', '["Penalty applies because $62,000 is less than 90% of the $75,000 current-year liability", "Penalty applies because Chambers made no estimated payments on partnership income", "No penalty because $62,000 withholding exceeds the 110% prior-year safe harbor of $60,500", "No penalty because the $13,000 balance due is under the de minimis threshold"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under Section 6654, no penalty applies if withholding and estimated payments meet either (a) 90% of current-year tax ($67,500) or (b) 110% of prior-year tax when prior-year AGI exceeds $150,000 ($55,000 × 110% = $60,500). Chambers'' $62,000 withholding exceeds the $60,500 prior-year safe harbor, so no penalty is owed despite the $13,000 underpayment. The de minimis exception applies only to balances under $1,000.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q20: medium / L2 — Care credit residency test (more than half year)
(3, 'Individual Taxation: Credits', 'Reynolds pays $5,000 in after-school care for her 8-year-old son in 2026. Due to a custody arrangement, the son lives with Reynolds from January through May (5 months) and with his father from June through December. Reynolds works full-time. Under Section 21, can Reynolds claim the child and dependent care credit?', '["Yes, because Reynolds paid the care expenses", "Yes, but only for the months the child lived with her", "No, because the credit is only available to married couples filing jointly", "No, because the child did not live with Reynolds for more than half the year"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under Section 21(b)(1), a qualifying individual for the child and dependent care credit must share the same principal place of abode with the taxpayer for more than half the tax year. Reynolds'' son lived with her for only 5 months, failing the residency test. The credit is not based solely on who paid the expenses. The credit is available to any eligible filing status, not just MFJ.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q21: easy / L1 — EIC earned income definition
(3, 'Individual Taxation: Credits', 'Under Section 32, what types of income count as earned income for the earned income credit?', '["Wages, salaries, tips, and net self-employment earnings", "All gross income including interest, dividends, and capital gains", "Only W-2 wages and salaries from an employer", "Wages plus any unemployment compensation received"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under Section 32(c)(2), earned income includes wages, salaries, tips, and net earnings from self-employment. Investment income (interest, dividends, capital gains), unemployment compensation, Social Security benefits, and pension distributions are excluded. The inclusion of self-employment income is significant because many EIC-eligible taxpayers earn income through gig work or freelancing rather than traditional W-2 employment.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q22: easy / L1 — Saver's Credit eligible retirement plan types
(3, 'Individual Taxation: Credits', 'Under Section 25B, contributions to which of the following qualify for the retirement savings contributions credit (Saver''s Credit)?', '["Only employer-sponsored 401(k) plans", "Only traditional and Roth IRAs", "Traditional IRAs, Roth IRAs, 401(k), 403(b), and governmental 457(b) plans", "Any savings account designated for retirement purposes"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under Section 25B(d), eligible contributions include those made to traditional IRAs, Roth IRAs, 401(k) plans, 403(b) plans, governmental 457(b) plans, SIMPLE IRAs, and SEP-IRAs. The credit covers a broad range of qualified retirement plans, not just employer plans or just IRAs. General savings accounts do not qualify because they are not tax-advantaged retirement plans under the Code.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q23: medium / L3 — CTC SSN timing requirement (by extended due date)
(3, 'Individual Taxation: Credits', 'Ortiz has a qualifying child born on November 15, 2026. Ortiz files his 2026 return on March 10, 2027, but does not obtain a Social Security number for the child until November 2027. Apply the identification requirements under Section 24(h)(7) to determine whether Ortiz can claim the child tax credit for 2026.', '["Yes, because the child was born during the tax year", "No, because the SSN must be issued by the due date of the return including extensions", "Yes, if Ortiz files an amended return after receiving the SSN", "No, because newborns are not eligible for the CTC in their birth year"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under Section 24(h)(7), a qualifying child must have an SSN issued on or before the due date of the return, including extensions (October 15, 2027 for 2026 returns). Since the SSN was not obtained until November 2027, after the extended due date, Ortiz cannot claim the $2,000 CTC. Ortiz may claim the $500 other dependent credit using an ITIN or ATIN. Filing an amended return does not cure the SSN timing deficiency.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q24: medium / L3 — AMT exemption phase-out calculation (25 cents per dollar)
(3, 'Individual Taxation: Credits', 'Foster, a single taxpayer, computes AMTI of $709,000 for 2026. The AMT exemption for single filers is $88,000, and the phase-out begins at $609,000. Apply the 25-cents-per-dollar phase-out rule under Section 55(d) to determine Foster''s remaining exemption.', '["$88,000", "$25,000", "$0", "$63,000"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under Section 55(d)(3), the AMT exemption is reduced by 25 cents for each dollar of AMTI above the phase-out threshold. Foster''s AMTI exceeds the $609,000 threshold by $100,000, producing a phase-out of $100,000 × 25% = $25,000. The remaining exemption is $88,000 − $25,000 = $63,000. The exemption would be fully eliminated at AMTI of $961,000 ($609,000 + $352,000). A common error is applying a 50% rate instead of 25%.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q25: easy / L1 — Premium Tax Credit refundability
(3, 'Individual Taxation: Credits', 'Under Section 36B, is the premium tax credit for marketplace health insurance refundable or nonrefundable?', '["Nonrefundable only", "Fully refundable", "Partially refundable, up to 40%", "Nonrefundable with a five-year carryforward"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under Section 36B, the premium tax credit is fully refundable, meaning it can reduce a taxpayer''s liability below zero and generate a refund. It can also be paid in advance directly to the insurance provider to reduce monthly premiums. The 40% partial refundability describes the AOTC, not the premium tax credit. Unlike nonrefundable credits such as the child and dependent care credit, the PTC ensures low-income taxpayers receive its full benefit.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q26: medium / L2 — Care credit for disabled dependent spouse
(3, 'Individual Taxation: Credits', 'Lawson''s spouse is physically incapable of self-care and lives with Lawson full-time in 2026. Lawson pays $4,000 for a home health aide to care for the spouse while Lawson works. Lawson''s AGI is $30,000. Under Section 21, can Lawson claim the child and dependent care credit for these expenses?', '["Yes, a spouse incapable of self-care qualifies as a dependent for the care credit", "No, the credit is only available for children under age 13", "No, because the expense is medical and must be claimed on Schedule A", "Yes, but only if the spouse has no income of their own"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under Section 21(b)(1)(C), a qualifying individual includes a spouse who is physically or mentally incapable of self-care and shares the taxpayer''s principal place of abode for more than half the year. The credit is not limited to children under 13—it also covers disabled dependents and spouses. The expenses must be work-related (to enable the taxpayer to work), not merely medical in nature.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q27: medium / L3 — AOTC qualified expenses (room and board excluded)
(3, 'Individual Taxation: Credits', 'Davis pays $12,000 in tuition, $1,500 in required textbooks, $8,000 in room and board, and $500 in required enrollment fees for his son''s first year at a university in 2026. His MAGI is $60,000 (single). Apply the qualified expense rules under Section 25A to determine the total expenses eligible for the AOTC.', '["$22,000", "$12,000", "$14,000", "$12,500"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under Section 25A(i)(3), AOTC qualified expenses include tuition, required enrollment fees, and course materials (books, supplies, and equipment needed for enrollment). Davis''s tuition ($12,000), required textbooks ($1,500), and required enrollment fees ($500) total $14,000 in qualifying expenses. Room and board ($8,000) is explicitly excluded from both education credits. The AOTC is calculated on up to $4,000 of these qualifying expenses, yielding the maximum $2,500 credit.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q28: medium / L3 — EIC tie-breaker rules (parent priority)
(3, 'Individual Taxation: Credits', 'Garcia, age 42, and her mother Reyes, age 65, both live in the same household with Garcia''s 10-year-old daughter in 2026. Both Garcia and Reyes claim the child as a qualifying child for the earned income credit. Apply the tie-breaker rules under Section 32(c)(1)(C) to determine who may claim the EIC for the child.', '["Reyes, because she is the older taxpayer", "The IRS splits the credit equally between Garcia and Reyes", "Either taxpayer may claim the credit regardless of relationship", "Garcia, because a parent takes priority over a non-parent under the tie-breaker rules"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under Section 152(c)(4) (incorporated by Section 32), when two or more taxpayers claim the same qualifying child, tie-breaker rules apply. A parent always takes priority over a non-parent. Since Garcia is the child''s parent and Reyes is the grandmother, Garcia has the superior claim. The age or AGI tie-breaker only applies when neither claimant is the child''s parent. The credit is never split between taxpayers.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q29: easy / L1 — Adoption credit maximum amount
(3, 'Individual Taxation: Credits', 'Under Section 23, what is the approximate maximum adoption credit per eligible child for 2026?', '["$10,000", "$14,440", "$16,810", "$20,000"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under Section 23, the maximum adoption credit for 2026 is approximately $16,810 per eligible child (indexed annually for inflation). This amount applies to both domestic and international adoptions. For special needs adoptions from U.S. foster care, the full credit is allowed regardless of actual expenses incurred. The credit is nonrefundable but may be carried forward for up to five years.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q30: easy / L1 — Estimated tax penalty is computed as interest
(3, 'Individual Taxation: Credits', 'Under Section 6654, what is the nature of the underpayment penalty for estimated taxes?', '["It is calculated as an interest charge on the underpaid amount, not a traditional penalty", "It is a flat 10% penalty on the total underpayment", "It is a criminal fine assessed by the IRS", "It is a percentage of the taxpayer''s total gross income"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under Section 6654, the estimated tax penalty is computed as an interest charge on the underpayment for the period of underpayment, using the federal short-term rate plus 3 percentage points. Unlike a flat penalty, it accrues based on the time and amount of the shortfall. It is not a criminal sanction and does not relate to gross income. The IRS may waive it in cases of casualty or disaster under Section 6654(e)(3).', 'easy', 1)
;

COMMIT;
